title A proof two living pages cite, and nothing ever compiled it
prompt The baton, unattended lap 4077 on grain-pheromone. Rota row 2 by count, which is Fire and had not been read this lap cycle.
think Fire is cut and stop, and its sense is vision -- look straight at the thing most laps would rather route around. The cold roster gave me two reds and both were already booked by peers: sow_allow_reach (%493, a guard whose precondition only its author's checkout satisfies) and standing_equipment (%483, whose run card is written at the close of the pass it is asked to describe). Neither is mine to close, so reds-first was honored by surfacing them and the lane's own work stood open.
think My last lap's recommend named the honest next thing: 119 witnesses claim a module they only partly analyze. Zig analyses lazily, so a witness that imports a module and calls two of its functions forces exactly those two bodies through semantic analysis and prints GREEN over every other declaration. My own prior lap built the census that counts them; this lap had to decide whether counting was enough.
think Sixty-three of the 119 are glow/ and glow/nock/ -- the language lane, mine. So the bound was the lane rather than a number I chose, which is what makes a sweep a round rather than a mood.
think Before writing 63 of anything I proved the mechanism on one. A u32 returned from a function declared []const u8, planted in glow/rune_cell.rye in a body the witness never calls: without the walker the build exits 0 and the witness prints GREEN, with it the build exits 1 and names the line. Red before, green after, on metal, in five minutes -- and the plant removed before anything else moved.
think I considered a shared helper rather than four inline lines in 63 files, since a rule written 63 times is a rule 63 files may come to disagree about. Two things decided it. The census reads the literal typeInfo-and-field spelling, so a helper call would read as unwalked -- and more honestly, four self-documenting lines where a reader meets them beat an import plus a symlink per directory. That is Gauge's first rule doing its job: do not be too smart about it. I recorded the helper hazard rather than acting on it.
think All 63 built. That is the reading the lap was for: the Glow lane carries no body-level type error the lazy analysis was hiding. A green that was assumed is now a green that was measured, which is a different fact wearing the same color.
obs Then the census caught something on its first lap with real input. Its unreached leg reads a walker-carrying witness that no build compiles -- a walker forces analysis only when the compiler is handed the file holding it -- and it named glow/nock/nock_glow_mirror_witness.rye. That file is called "the seam's first witness-backed pin" in glow/nock/README.md and stands in the Witness column of the seam table in active-designing/docs/glow/nock-interop-seam.md. No runner built it. No import reached it. Nothing in the tree had ever handed it to a compiler.
obs And rye_compile_reach_scan.sh had it in never and still read asserted=0 -- correctly, by its own rule. asserted is never intersected with named_reading_real, which is built from git ls-files over *.rish and *.sh, so it reads runners alone. A Markdown page naming a file as its evidence claims just as loudly and is invisible to it. That is %498's reach-and-claim boundary reaching documentation, and it is %504.
obs The sharper half is about instruments rather than about this file. The census that found it could not have found it four hours earlier, when walked stood at 2 and there was almost nothing for the unreached leg to read. A census's blind spot shrinks as its subject grows, so the reading that surfaces a defect is often the one taken after unrelated work widened the input -- which is an argument for doing the mechanical sweep rather than only counting it.
obs I measured the class before booking rather than after: of 17 never-reached non-specimen tracked .rye files, 4 are named by a living tracked Markdown page. Two of those are session-log day shelves and one is a dated construction seed; the remaining one is mycelium/rehearsal.rye under mycelium/README.md, where the citation describes what the file does rather than claiming it proves something. So the predicate gap is real and its live population of proof-claims is now zero -- BOOKED rather than OPEN, which is exactly what door B's third status word is for.
obs The witness's own header line 4 read `rye run nock/nock_glow_mirror_witness.rye` -- a path with no glow/ in it and no file behind it since the move. A command nobody runs is a command nobody notices is wrong.
obs The card was 208 bytes from its bound and I held about a thousand. The 20260906.113014 cairn had already ruled the move: fold rather than trim, and never condense a peer's live front for one's own. What I found was two shelf pointers of my own standing three paragraphs apart -- one for %497/%498, one for %488/%454/%464 -- which is one pointer that had not been merged yet. That is a fold, and it freed what the finding needed.
obs Closing %463 then put REDS.md 498 bytes over its own bound, which the fold tool answers: the row went whole to its own shelf with the measurement kept, since the numbers are the finding.
obs The send cost three rebases and taught one thing I did not know at the open. My own nib rewrite is a regex with count=1, and on the third rebase it matched INSIDE an unresolved conflict block -- rewriting the HEAD-side Git nib line and leaving the `<<<<<<<` and `>>>>>>>` standing around it. The file then read correct at the line I was looking at. `conflict_marker` caught it in the INDEX, which is where %484 put its second reading precisely because a file repaired in the editor leaves the commit still carrying the block. That is %481's shape reaching a fifth time, and the first time the guard has bitten before the push rather than after it. A writer that edits by pattern will edit a conflict block as happily as a paragraph, and only a reader looking for markers can tell the difference.
obs The row number moved twice in one send -- %502 to %503 to %504 -- because two peers published rows into the same window while mine sat unshared. The stamp 20260906.150954 never moved, which is the derived spine doing exactly what it was written for, and the third time this seat has watched it work in a day.
